WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.029
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:10.640
This research group is known for their extraordinary
ideas, and this piece of work is, of course,

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:16.330
no exception. This paper is about time varying
textures. Have a look at these photographs

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:21.490
that were taken at a different time. And the
million dollar question is, can we simulate

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:27.939
how this texture would look if we were to
go forward in time? A texture weathering simulation,

00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:31.859
if you will. The immediate answer is that
of course not.

00:00:31.859 --> 00:00:38.110
However, in this piece of work, a single input
image is taken, and without any user interaction,

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:44.440
the algorithm attempts to understand how this
texture might have looked in the past. Now,

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:49.460
let's start out by addressing the elephant
in the room: this problem can obviously not

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:55.520
be solved in the general case for any image.
However, if we restrict our assumptions to

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.970
textures that contain a repetitive pattern,
then it is much more feasible to identify

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:02.650
the weathering patterns.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:08.450
To achieve this, an age map is built where
the red regions show the parts that are assumed

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:13.350
to be weathered. You can see on the image
how these weathering patterns break up the

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:18.409
regularity. Leaning on the assumption that
if we go back in time, the regions marked

00:01:18.409 --> 00:01:24.429
with red will recede, and if we go forward
in time, they will grow, we can write a really

00:01:24.429 --> 00:01:29.369
cool weathering simulator that creates results
that look like wizardry.

00:01:29.369 --> 00:01:35.420
Broken glass, cracks, age rings on a wooden
surface, you name it.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:45.459
But we can also use this technique to transfer
weathering patterns from one image onto another.

00:01:45.459 --> 00:01:50.740
Textures with multiple layers are also supported,
which means that it can handle images that

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:57.200
are given as a sum of a regular and irregular
patterns. The blue background is regular and

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:03.189
quite symmetric, but the "no parking" text
is lacking these regularities. And the amazing

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:07.139
thing is that the technique still works on
such cases.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:13.780
The results are also demonstrated by putting
these weathered textures on 3D models so we

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:17.060
can see them all in their glory in our own
application.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:27.140
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

